## INTRODUCTION
As an irreplaceable feedstock for global agriculture and industry,1 ammonia is produced at a rate of 140 million metric tons per year, making it among the largest volume chemicals on the planet. Although critical to our food supply and economy, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is a highly toxic gas even in small concentrations. Therefore, sorbents capable of removing trace $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ are of interest for industrial air remediation, personal protective equipment, as well as separation of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ from $\Nu_{2}$ and ${\bf{\dot{H}}}_{2}$ .3,4 For these varied applications, uptake at low $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ concentrations is required, yet the current industrial standard sorbents, activated carbons, suffer from relatively low affinity as well as limited capacity for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ .5,6 More fundamentally, the heterogeneity of pore sizes in carbons has prevented systematic studies of ammonia sorption in microporous materials, which are crucial for improved adsorbent designs.
To address this need, recent efforts have focused on developing sorbents with Lewis or Brønsted acidic active sites that impart higher affinity for the basic $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecule.8,9 Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) with coordinatively unsaturated, Lewis acidic open metal sites such as $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{trimesate})_{2}$ (HKUST-1), $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(2,5-$ dihydroxybenzenedicarboxylate) $(\mathrm{Mg\mathrm{-}}$ MOF-74), and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}$ (isonicotinic acid) have emerged as superlative dry $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorbents. However, their performance almost invariably suffers upon repeated exposure to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and, more critically, they often degrade upon exposure to water vapor.10–
12 Porous polymers with a high density of Brønsted acid sites also demonstrate superior $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ sorption,9,13 their stable allcarbon backbone offering distinct advantages for performance under humid conditions. However, the heterogeneous pore size distribution in these porous organic polymers impedes studies of pore size effects on sorption kinetics. Further, the interpenetrated nature of the porous polymers leads to a highly tortuous pore network with small windows, which substantially retards gas uptake. Indeed, although a high maximum $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacity is desirable, increasing the uptake often comes at the expense of slower sorption kinetics. For instance, nonporous $\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}$ has the greatest capacity for ammonia of any known material, but slow kinetics as well as a large volume expansion upon $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake limit its utility in many applications.14 Despite its obvious importance, the kinetics of ammonia sorption in porous materials is seldom investigated. Reports of sorbent performance overwhelmingly focus on uptake capacity with no mention of adsorption kinetics or, at best, indirect investigations through the analysis of gas breakthrough experiments.13,15
Recently, we reported a series of particularly robust MOFs, $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}(\mathbf{BTDD})$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Co, Ni, Cu; B $\mathrm{\hbar\mathrm{TDD}}=\mathrm{bis}(1H-1,2,3$ - triazolo[4,5-b], $[4^{\prime},5^{\prime}-\mathrm{i}],$ dibenzo[1,4]dioxin),16,17 which feature large hexagonal mesoporous channels with a diameter of 2.3 nm lined with chains of Lewis acidic metals that exhibit open coordination sites (Figure 1). These materials were the first examples of MOFs that were stable to repeated sorption and desorption of ammonia. Their extreme stability to corrosive gases was further highlighted by repeated exposure to chlorine and bromine gas, which can also be stored reversibly in these materials over multiple cycles.16,18 Here, we utilize these materials and their smaller-pore isoreticular analogs, $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BB}\mathbf{TA}$ $\mathrm{~(~M~)~}=\mathrm{~\Co~}$ , Ni, Cu; BBTA $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $1H,5H.$ -benzo(1,2-d),(4,5- d′)bistriazole)19,20 as a ready-made platform for interrogating the relationships between pore size and active site density on the total uptake as well as the kinetics of ammonia sorption. We show that the higher capacity for ammonia in the smallerpore materials cannot be accounted for simply by the increase in the density of open metal sites. Instead, cooperative proximity effects likely lead to the anomalous observed increase in ammonia saturation uptake for $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BBTA}$ . Using InfraSORP,21–23 a recently developed calorimetric technique, we further draw correlations between pore size and sorption kinetics. Finally, we show that MOF stability to ammonia or water, an oft-empirical parameter of fundamental and practical importance, correlates directly with the water exchange rates in homoleptic metal-aquo complexes, a fundamental property established decades $\mathrm{ago}^{24}$ that will serve as an easy guide for new MOF design in the future.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Ammonia sorption experiments for activated samples of $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BB}\mathbf{TA}$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{M}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{C}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{0}}$ , Ni, Cu) at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ revealed type I isotherms, with steep uptakes at low absolute pressure (Figure 2). The total $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptakes at 1 bar were 17.95, 14.68, and 19.79 mmol $\mathrm{NH_{3}\ {g}^{-1}}$ for the Co, Ni, and $\mathrm{cu}$ analogs, respectively. Compared to the larger pore BTDD materials, the uptakes observed for BBTA materials are considerably higher,16 positioning $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ as the material with the highest $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake among all MOFs. Intriguingly, the increase in gravimetric capacity in going from $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BTDD}$ to the smaller pore $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BB}\mathbf{TA}$ is greater than what would be expected from simply increasing the density of active open metal sites. For instance, the equilibrium uptake of 2.72 molecules of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ per Co atom at 1 bar and $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ in mesoporous $\mathrm{Co_{2}C l_{2}B T D D^{16}}$ increases to 3.20 molecules of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ per Co atom in microporous $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ . We attribute this anomalous increase in ammonia density to the shorter distance between neighboring chains of metal-bound $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ . This may allow for increased hydrogen bonding interactions between the first, metal-bound $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules and additional $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorbed in the pore to an extent that is not available in the larger-pore $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}\mathbf{BTDD}$ series. In support of this hypothesis, we note that a similar confinement effect is operative for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake in microporous zeolites.25
After exposure to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ BBTA retains its crystallinity and porosity (Figures S4.1 and S5.2), whereas $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ becomes amorphous and non-porous (Figures S4.2, and S5.3) both in line with the behavior observed for the respective larger pore analogs (for $\mathrm{Cu_{2}C l_{2}B T D D^{26}}$ Figures S3.2, S4.3 and S5.4).16  By contrast, post-ammonia powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) measurements indicate that $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ , unlike its larger pore analog, loses much of its crystallinity upon exposure to 1 bar ammonia (Figure S4.4), which also leads to increased hysteresis during desorption (Figure 2) and substantially reduced BrunauerEmmett Teller (BET) surface area (Figure S5.5, Table S5.1). Although $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ collapses upon exposure to 1 bar $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , it remains stable and retains its porosity at 1 mbar $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , conditions that are more relevant for personal protection equipment (Figures S4.4, S5.5). Furthermore, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ cycling experiments revealed that despite its collapse at higher ammonia pressure, $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ retains a substantial uptake upon cycling with reactivation at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . These results mirror those observed for mesoporous $\mathrm{Mn_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ (Figure S3.1) and are in line with other studies that have shown that a decrease in surface area does not predict a decline in the ammonia uptake; rather, the interaction strength between bonding sites and ammonia is a more important predictor of ammonia uptake.9,27 Although the total surface area decreases, the open metal sites that strongly bind ammonia evidently remain accessible.
The different stability observed for $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ under identical conditions was intriguing because in the vast majority of MOFs where the primary coordination sphere of the metal is conserved, as here, it is the larger pore material that undergoes pore collapse more readily. To investigate this anomalous behavior, we measured a series of variable temperature ammonia isotherms for the larger pore $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ . More pronounced hystereses became evident as the isotherm temperature dropped below $298\ \mathrm{K}$ , and at $263~\mathrm{K}$ we observed a similar hysteresis in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ as in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ (Figure 3). As with the small pore analog, this lower temperature $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ isotherm also led to partial structural collapse and decreased crystallinity (Figure S4.5). These experiments showed that pore collapse and hysteresis are closely correlated with the local concentration of ammonia in the pore. The cobalt frameworks are stable when ammonia uptake is limited at and around the open metal sites in a layer/cluster mechanism, but if the pores of these materials start filling with ammonia, which begins to occur at room temperature for the small-pore analog and at lower temperature for the large-pore material, the crystallinity suffers. With $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ , ammonia sorption remains quasi-reversible even at $263\mathrm{~K~}$ although the isotherm profile changes relative to the $298{\mathrm{~K~}}$ isotherm, with the $298\mathrm{~K~}$ capacity reached at only 0.4 bar, followed by a pronounced step increase and saturation at 35 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 bar (Figure 3). The increase in uptake followed by saturation is characteristic of pore-filling behavior, observed previously in these systems for water adsorption.17 Importantly, $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ retains its crystallinity and high surface area even after pore filling (Figures S4.6 and S5.6) and represents, to our knowledge, the first example of reversible ammonia pore-filling behavior in a MOF, of potential interest for adsorption heat pumps.28
## Simple kinetic descriptors define MOF stability towards polar coordinating molecules
Calorimetric measurements and theoretical calculations have shown that porous phases of MOFs are almost universally metastable, that is, uphill in energy with respect to their dense phases.29,30 Finding the more stable structures in this class has largely been empirical, but ultimately, as with any material, stabilization of a metastable porous MOF phase relative to the dense phase can only be kinetic. It must involve raising the reaction coordinate energy barrier between the two phases, either by decreasing the energy of the porous phase, or by increasing the transition state energy. The former is more intuitive: extensive previous work has shown that increasing the metal-ligand bond strength, generally the weakest link in MOF structures, results in enhanced stability toward polar analytes, e.g. water and ammonia, a result of lowering the energy of the porous frameworks.31–34 Similarly, the triazolate frameworks investigated here already exhibit enhanced stability over their carboxylate containing counterparts such as MOF-74.35,36 Here, we further posit that the stability trend within this family of triazolate MOFs follows the kinetic metal-aquo substitution rate, an effect of the transition state energy as a result of the size of the metal ions. The $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ materials are most stable towards ammonia: the small pore $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C l_{2}B B T A}$ framework is the only one to retain its surface area after exposure to 1 bar $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , and the large-pore $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ withstands pore filling with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . The ${\mathrm{Co}}^{\bar{2}+}$ materials both withstand layercluster ammonia binding, but not pore filling, whereas the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ materials degrade even at low concentrations of ammonia. This stability trend is in line with the experimentally observed kinetic metal-aquo substitution rate. $\mathrm{Ni}^{\hat{2}+}$ is two orders of magnitude more kinetically inert than ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , which, in turn, is approximately two orders of magnitude more kinetically inert than either $\dot{\bf M}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ .24 We note that kinetic stability has also been invoked to explain the exceptional robustness of the carboxylate-based ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ -containing MIL-101.37,38 Notably, in the MIL-53 and -47 family of isostructural frameworks, cation inertness is a more relevant predictor of stability: chemical robustness decreases in the order $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}>\mathrm{Al}^{3+}>\mathrm{V}^{\bar{4+}}$ , in line with the water substitution rates in the respective aquo complexes, but diverging from the thermodynamic trend in metaloxygen bond strength. Further, even partial replacement of $\mathrm{Mg}^{\overline{{{2}^{+}}}}$ in Mg-MOF-74 with more inert $\mathrm{\hat{N}i}^{2+}$ ions significantly enhances the stability of the $\mathbf{Mg}$ material.40 Although incorporation of kinetically inert metals into MOFs can be challenging synthetically,41,42 future efforts in this direction can provide a route towards enhancing chemical robustness.
## Pore size effects on breakthrough performance and kinetics
The small- and large-pore cobalt frameworks contain identical, stable, strongly adsorptive active sites which are regularly spaced through one-dimensional pores of varying diameters, providing an ideal platform to investigate the effects of pore size on $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ breakthrough performance as well as kinetics. Dynamic breakthrough measurements at $1000\mathrm{\ppm\NH_{3}}$ revealed that $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ has a record dry capacity (uptake until $1000\ \mathrm{ppm}$ is reached) of $8.56~\mathrm{mmol~g}^{-1}$ , $27\%$ higher than the state-of-the-art HKUST-1,43 while the larger-pore $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ has a capacity of $4.78\ \mathrm{\mmol\g^{-1}}$ (Figure 4A, Table 1). These breakthrough capacities are equal to 1.48 molecules of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ per $C_{0}$ atom for the small-pore material and 1.08 molecules per open Co site for the large-pore material. Interestingly, the breakthrough criterion for ammonia (5 ppm) is reached after the small-pore material has adsorbed 6.24 mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and the large-pore MOF has adsorbed 4.42 mmol $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}\ \mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , corresponding to 1.08 and 1.00 molecules of ammonia per open metal site respectively. These results demonstrate that increasing the density of open metal sites results in a linear increase in the breakthrough performance, while the saturation capacity is further enhanced in the small pore material, in line with the proximity effects observed in the static isotherms discussed above. In the dry breakthrough curves, the $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ concentration remains at the baseline until all metal sites have been filled, then the downstream concentration begins to rise (Figure 4A). The steep increase in downstream $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ concentration for the large-pore material further evidences weaker secondary interactions compared with the shallow sigmoidal rise in concentration for the small-pore material.
Expectedly, $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ breakthrough measurements performed at $80\%$ relative humidity (RH) revealed that both small-pore $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ and large-pore $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ are affected by moisture (Figure 4B, Table 1). $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ retains a saturation capacity of $4.36\mathrm{\mol\kg^{-1}}$ whereas $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ has a humid saturation capacity of $\overline{{3.38}}~\mathrm{mol~kg}^{-1}$ . These values correspond to 0.76 and $0.77~\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules per open metal site respectively, a decrease from the dry case that is clearly due to competitive adsorption with water.17 These humid breakthrough capacities remain superior to conventional porous materials such as zeolites and activated carbons,5 and show promise for air purification applications.
The breakthrough experiments demonstrate that the kinetics of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake, obviously vital for both personal protection and gas separations applications,44 are not easily predicted by static isotherm measurements, although many studies of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ adsorbents focus solely on capacity. We interrogated the kinetics of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake in $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ 2BTDD using InfraSORP, an optical calorimetric method.21,23,22 Measurements revealed that the large-pore material has significantly more rapid ammonia uptake (Figure 5A). Fitting of the initial $500\mathrm{~s~}$ of calorimetric data to two exponentials according to the equation $T=T_{o}((1-e^{-k_{1}t})-(1-e^{-k_{2}t}))$ showed firstorder rate constants, $k_{I},$ for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ uptake of $\boldsymbol{0.{\dot{4}}2\ {\mathrm{s}}^{-1}}$ and $0.28~\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ for the large-pore and the small-pore Co frameworks, respectively (Figures S7.1 and S7.2). This is not surprising: $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ molecules are able to more swiftly reach all open Co sites in $\mathrm{Co_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ .
The optical calorimetric method assumes that the two materials have equivalent heats of adsorption for ammonia and similar heat capacities. To test these assumptions, the areas under the kinetics curves were integrated and multiplied by the sample masses, providing an estimate for the energy absorbed into the materials (Figures S7.3-S7.6), which should correlate with the amount of ammonia adsorbed, as confirmed previously in other systems.21–23 The ratio of heat transferred to the small pore versus the large pore as determined by InfraSORP was 1.49, in excellent agreement with the ratio of 1.48 for equilibrium $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ capacities at 1 mbar determined from low pressure $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ isotherms (Figure S3.4), validating the applicability of the InfraSORP method for ammonia.
The kinetic data provides predictive power when combined with relevant models for gas diffusion, such as the Knudsen model.45 The latter applies when the mean free path of the analyte molecule is larger than the pore diameter of the sorbent.45 Here, the mean free path for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with a kinetic diameter of $0.326~\mathrm{nm}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 1 bar is $871\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , indeed much greater than either of the pore diameters of our materials, 13 and $23\textup{\AA}$ . In these scenarios, the flux from gas-phase ammonia to an intra-pore adsorption site can be approximated as that for a circular tube with rigid, non-adsorbing walls, and is given by:
$$
F=\frac{1}{3}\sqrt{8/_{\pi\mathrm{M}R T}}\frac{d\varepsilon}{L\tau}
$$
where $M$ is the molecular weight of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , $R$ is the gas constant, $T$ 𝑇 is the temperature, $d$ is the pore diameter, $\varepsilon$ is the void fraction, $L$ is the length of the path from gas phase to the inner adsorption site, and $\tau$ is the tortuosity. Because $M,R,T$ 𝑇, and $\tau$ are identical in both MOFs, the flux ratio for large pore (L) to small pore (S) materials is:
$$
\frac{F_{L}}{F_{S}}=\frac{d_{L}\varepsilon_{L}L_{S}}{d_{S}\varepsilon_{S}L_{L}}
$$
Using pore diameters determined by Barrett-JoynerHalenda46,47 theory of $13\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and $23\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Figure S5.1), and crystallographic void fractions of $0.4403^{\dot{2}0}$ and $0.6266^{16}$ for the $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ , respectively, we would expect the ratio of uptake rates (i.e. flux ratio) between the large𝐿𝑆𝐿 𝐿. The ratio $L_{\mathrm{S}}/L_{\mathrm{L}}$ is difficult to quantify because the average diffusional distance to each active site depends on particle length and aspect ratio, as well as differences in active site density. As such, $L_{\mathrm{S}}/L_{\mathrm{L}}$ was estimated by scanning electron microscopy (Figures S6.1 and S6.2), which showed that crystallites of the small pore material were on average half the size of those observed for the large pore MOF, allowing us to approximate $L_{\mathrm{S}}/L_{\mathrm{L}}$ as $\sim0.5$ . Overall, the expected flux ratio $F_{\mathrm{L}}/F_{\mathrm{S}}$ was thus expected to be approximately 1.25, in good agreement with the experimentally determined ratio of rate constants of 1.5.
Altogether, the kinetic data show that the Knudsen model serves as a reasonable baseline for understanding and predicting the effects of modifying the pore size and the crystallite size on the speed of gas uptake in MOFs, even when the analytes are strongly interacting gases, and that decreasing particle size can mitigate the slower uptake kinetics resulting from reducing the pore size. Future research aimed at uncovering the kinetics of gas sorption in MOFs in finer detail will further aid prediction of material suitability for varied applications including personal protective equipment, separations, and heat pumps.
## CONCLUSIONS
Stable triazolate frameworks containing open metal sites provide a unique platform for interrogating coordinating and corrosive gas sorption. Due to their higher density of open metal sites, the smaller-pore BBTA materials have significantly greater capacities than their larger-pore counterparts, with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ exhibiting the greatest static uptake at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ of any MOF or porous organic polymer. Additionally, increasing the density of open metal sites results in a linear increase in breakthrough performance, as the breakthrough criteria for $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , $5\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , is reached after one molecule of ammonia is adsorbed per open metal site in both small-pore $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ and large-pore $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ . Increasing the density of open metal sites results in a record dry dynamic ammonia capacity for $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BBTA}$ at $1000\mathrm{\ppm}$ of 8.56 mmol $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , exceeding that of the state-of-the-art HKUST-1 by $27\%$ . Decreasing the pore size from 23 to $13\mathrm{~\AA~}$ results in slower sorption kinetics, though this effect can be mitigated by synthesizing smaller MOF particles. Kinetic analyses correlate with the Knudsen flux model, demonstrating that this theoretical framework can be used as a baseline to predict the effects of modifying the pore size as well as the particle size on sorbent kinetic performance. Further, the stability trend of the isoreticular frameworks substituted with various metal ions follows the metal-aquo substitution rate, with the large-pore framework formed with the most inert metal, $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C l_{2}B T D D}$ , able to withstand pore filling with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ at low temperature. This work provides a clear path towards designer sorbents by controlling the uptake, kinetics, and stability of the active materials.
